WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.870
Back to the bank.

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.585
Last time, one key guarded every balance, so only one transfer could run at a time.

3
00:00:09.585 --> 00:00:10.617
Safe, but slow.

4
00:00:10.617 --> 00:00:15.505
A transfer between two friends waited behind one between two strangers.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:19.736
So now each account gets its own small safe, with its own key.

6
00:00:19.736 --> 00:00:23.481
A transfer takes both keys it needs, then moves the money.

7
00:00:23.481 --> 00:00:27.743
Transfers between different accounts can finally run side by side.

8
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:32.351
Meet Ana and Ben, two friends who keep paying each other back.

9
00:00:32.351 --> 00:00:34.826
Ana's account is a, and Ben's is b.

10
00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:36.805
Ana sends Ben 100 for lunch.

11
00:00:36.805 --> 00:00:40.836
At the very same moment, Ben sends Ana 50 for the cinema.

12
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:44.832
Each transfer is correct on its own, and both pass every test.

13
00:00:44.832 --> 00:00:47.201
So here's a question that sounds silly.

14
00:00:47.201 --> 00:00:49.812
Can two correct transfers break each other?

15
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.073
Let's slow it down.

16
00:00:54.073 --> 00:00:56.886
Ana's transfer takes its first key, key a.

17
00:00:56.886 --> 00:01:01.039
At the same moment, Ben's transfer takes its first key, key b.

18
00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:05.192
Now Ana's transfer reaches for key b, but Ben's is holding it.

19
00:01:05.192 --> 00:01:09.345
And Ben's transfer reaches for key a, but Ana's is holding it.

20
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:13.849
Each one gives back its first key only after it gets its second.

21
00:01:13.849 --> 00:01:15.255
So neither ever will.

22
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:16.132
No error.

23
00:01:16.132 --> 00:01:17.018
No log line.

24
00:01:17.018 --> 00:01:18.865
The processor goes quiet.

25
00:01:18.865 --> 00:01:24.923
Java threads are endlessly patient: they'll wait forever, and never once complain.

26
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:28.848
This is a deadlock: threads waiting for each other forever.

27
00:01:28.848 --> 00:01:32.751
Both sit in BLOCKED, the state of a thread waiting for a lock.

28
00:01:32.751 --> 00:01:36.780
And every other transfer that needs a or b piles up behind them.

29
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.701
The nasty part is timing.

30
00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:46.322
Both first keys must be taken in the same tiny window, so a test that runs one transfer at a time never sees it.

31
00:01:46.322 --> 00:01:49.180
On a busy day, it's only a matter of when.

32
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.015
So what does a freeze need?

33
00:01:51.015 --> 00:01:53.765
Read the picture, and you'll find four things.

34
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:54.289
One.

35
00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:57.025
Each key has one holder at a time.

36
00:01:57.025 --> 00:01:58.957
That's mutual exclusion.

37
00:01:59.167 --> 00:01:59.462
Two.

38
00:01:59.462 --> 00:02:03.308
A transfer holds one key while it waits for another.

39
00:02:03.308 --> 00:02:04.861
That's hold and wait.

40
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:05.504
Three.

41
00:02:05.504 --> 00:02:08.635
Nobody can take a key away from its holder.

42
00:02:08.635 --> 00:02:10.164
That's no preemption.

43
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:10.725
Four.

44
00:02:10.725 --> 00:02:17.179
The waiting goes round in a circle: Ana's transfer waits for Ben's, which waits for Ana's.

45
00:02:17.179 --> 00:02:18.685
That's circular wait.

46
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.062
A deadlock needs all four at once.

47
00:02:21.062 --> 00:02:23.287
Break any one, and it can't happen.

48
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.996
The cheapest one to break is the circle.

49
00:02:25.996 --> 00:02:30.426
A circle needs two transfers that disagree about which key comes first.

50
00:02:30.426 --> 00:02:31.985
So let's make them agree.

51
00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.855
Every account has a number that never changes.

52
00:02:34.855 --> 00:02:39.597
The rule: always take the lower number first, whichever way the money moves.

53
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.792
Now both transfers reach for key a first.

54
00:02:42.792 --> 00:02:45.128
Ana's gets it, then takes key b.

55
00:02:45.128 --> 00:02:47.975
Ben's waits for key a, holding nothing.

56
00:02:47.975 --> 00:02:49.799
That's just taking turns.

57
00:02:49.799 --> 00:02:51.332
No circle, no freeze.

58
00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:59.763
Let's test it on the exact traffic that froze the bank: 200,000 transfers between the same two accounts, half each way.

59
00:02:59.763 --> 00:03:01.147
Will it ever freeze?

60
00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:10.977
With the lock order, every run finished, in 33 to 50 milliseconds, and the total stayed at 2,000,000.

61
00:03:10.977 --> 00:03:15.787
With the keys taken in argument order, it hung on five runs out of six.

62
00:03:15.787 --> 00:03:17.346
The sixth run finished.

63
00:03:17.346 --> 00:03:19.243
That's the same bug, hiding.

64
00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:21.211
Two details make or break it.

65
00:03:21.211 --> 00:03:25.483
The number must be unique and never change, so an account number works.

66
00:03:25.483 --> 00:03:28.191
And every piece of code must follow the rule.

67
00:03:28.191 --> 00:03:32.342
One method that takes the keys the other way builds the circle again.

68
00:03:32.342 --> 00:03:36.012
So write the rule once, in one helper, and use it everywhere.

69
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:39.444
Sometimes you can't know both keys up front.

70
00:03:39.444 --> 00:03:44.065
Then break a different condition, no preemption, with a lock that gives up.

71
00:03:44.065 --> 00:03:49.178
ReentrantLock has tryLock, which waits at most, say, 10 milliseconds, then says no.

72
00:03:49.178 --> 00:03:55.277
If a transfer can't get its second key in time, it puts the first one back, rests, and tries again.

73
00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:01.602
But what if both transfers give up at the same moment, rest just as long, and try again together?

74
00:04:01.602 --> 00:04:04.496
Picture two very polite diners with one spoon.

75
00:04:04.496 --> 00:04:07.516
Each hands it over whenever the other is hungry.

76
00:04:07.516 --> 00:04:09.969
How many meals get eaten in one second?

77
00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:17.956
In the demo, the spoon changed hands about 6 million times in one second.

78
00:04:17.956 --> 00:04:18.708
Nobody ate.

79
00:04:18.708 --> 00:04:22.740
That's a livelock: everyone is busy, and nothing gets done.

80
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.791
Perfect manners, empty plates.

81
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.972
The fix is to break the step.

82
00:04:26.972 --> 00:04:29.896
Rest for a random time before trying again.

83
00:04:29.896 --> 00:04:33.433
Or add a rule: after three polite turns, eat anyway.

84
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:38.125
With that rule, the spoon changed hands 6 times, and both diners ate.

85
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:41.604
One last way to get stuck is starvation.

86
00:04:41.604 --> 00:04:45.744
The program moves, but one unlucky thread never gets its turn.

87
00:04:45.744 --> 00:04:50.217
A fair lock serves the longest waiter first, at some cost in speed.

88
00:04:51.033 --> 00:04:55.625
Here's the lock order in code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

89
00:04:55.625 --> 00:04:58.878
Pick the account with the lower id, then the other.

90
00:04:58.878 --> 00:05:00.408
Lock them in that order.

91
00:05:00.408 --> 00:05:01.684
Then move the money.

92
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.496
So, can two correct transfers break each other?

93
00:05:05.496 --> 00:05:08.746
Yes, if they take the same keys in opposite orders.

94
00:05:08.746 --> 00:05:11.997
A deadlock needs four things at once, so break one.

95
00:05:11.997 --> 00:05:16.713
The cheapest is the circle: one lock order, written once, used everywhere.

96
00:05:16.933 --> 00:05:17.729
Quick check.

97
00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:20.913
One method locks the customer, then the account.

98
00:05:20.913 --> 00:05:23.899
Another locks the account, then the customer.

99
00:05:23.899 --> 00:05:24.960
Can they freeze?
